# UI Design System — Vercel Style

When working on frontend UI, follow a Vercel-like design language.

## Visual direction

- Clean, minimal, calm, product-focused
- High clarity, low decoration
- Finance-friendly and enterprise-safe
- Prefer whitespace over dense layouts

## Typography

- Use Geist (already in project) as the primary font
- Strong hierarchy:
  - Page titles: clear and restrained
  - Section headers: medium weight
  - Body text: readable, not heavy
- Avoid oversized display typography
- Avoid playful or marketing-style fonts

## Color

- Neutral-first palette (zinc/stone/neutral)
- Black/white/gray foundation
- One subtle accent only
- Avoid loud gradients, neon, or heavy color blocks
- Borders should be soft and low-contrast

## Components

- Prefer shadcn/ui primitives
- Cards with subtle borders, not heavy shadows
- Rounded corners: modest (not pill-heavy)
- Buttons:
  - Primary: solid, simple
  - Secondary: outline/ghost
- Tables and lists should feel like Vercel dashboard density
- Status badges: quiet and compact

## Layout

- Consistent max content width
- Clear page header + optional description
- Predictable spacing scale
- Avoid cluttered side panels unless necessary
- Prefer simple split views over complex multi-panel dashboards

## Interaction

- Fast and obvious
- Minimal animation
- No decorative motion
- Feedback states should be clear but quiet

## Do NOT

- Make it look like a consumer consumer AI toy
- Use glassmorphism, neon glows, or heavy gradients
- Overuse icons
- Create inconsistent button styles
- Invent a new design language

When redesigning UI, match the restraint and polish of Vercel dashboard / Next.js docs / shadcn examples.
